window.AMETHERIAN_SEARCH = [{"path":"/","title":"A new kind of Node library ecosystem","section":"Home","description":"Ametherian is an independent Node.js library ecosystem: focused JavaScript and TypeScript packages, shared contracts, and security-conscious engineering.","headings":[],"text":"Independent Node.js Ecosystem A new kind of Node library ecosystem. Ametherian is a proposed family of modular JavaScript and TypeScript libraries under the @foulfoxhacks npm scope. The direction: focused packages, shared contracts, and security considered from the first design decision. Read the vision → Explore packages Ametherian A new kind of Node library ecosystem Project status Packages Proposed Implementations Not published Security audit Not performed Node.js-first JavaScript · TypeScript · npm Overview One ecosystem. Independently adoptable tools. Node.js developers assemble networking, validation, caching, concurrency, observability, and application infrastructure from many packages. Ametherian investigates where consistent contracts and well-defined package boundaries could make that work easier to understand and maintain. Each future package should have a clear purpose, documented behavior, explicit limits, and the freedom to be adopted on its own. The ambition is broader than replacing a single HTTP client. Read the project vision → Package Map Five core areas. The package map is a research agenda. Each family describes work worth investigating. Read the package map . Networking and protocols HTTP clients, transports, connection pools, proxies, streaming, retries, deadlines, and cancellation. ↗ Data and serialization Validation, schemas, parsing, serialization, conversion, structured data, and safe handling of untrusted input. ↗ Application infrastructure Caching, queues, concurrency, rate limiting, configuration, lifecycle management, and resilience. ↗ Securit"},{"path":"/docs/","title":"Documentation","section":"Documentation","description":"Read the Ametherian documentation: project vision, package map, architecture direction, security approach, and roadmap.","headings":[{"id":"start","text":"Start here #","level":2},{"id":"policy","text":"Project policy #","level":2},{"id":"status","text":"Status at a glance #","level":2},{"id":"conventions","text":"How to read the documentation #","level":2},{"id":"records","text":"Project records #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation Documentation Documentation Vision, package map, architecture, security, and roadmap. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Status. Ametherian is a public planning project. Nothing in this documentation is a release announcement. Package names and capabilities are research directions, not published software. Start here # Read the documentation in order if you are evaluating the direction. Each document states what is planned, what is not yet known, and what evidence would count as progress. Document Project vision Why an ecosystem, what is in scope, and the boundaries the project will respect. Read → Document Package map Five research areas and candidate package names. Read → Document Architecture direction Modular packages, shared contracts, runtime boundaries, and resource safety. Read → Document Security approach Threat modeling, secure engineering practices, and vulnerability intelligence. Read → Document Roadmap The staged path from research to release readiness. Read → Project policy # Policy documents describe how the project handles security reports and contributions. Policy Security policy Current reporting status, coordinated disclosure, and the intended security process. Read → Policy Contributing How to give feedback, standards, and pull request expectations. Read → Status at a glance # The table below is the project’s own status report. A row moves forward only when there is public evidence. Ametherian project status by area Area Current status What would establish progress Project vision Drafted Maintainer review and community feedb"},{"path":"/docs/vision/","title":"Project vision","section":"Proposal document","description":"The Ametherian project vision: a family of focused JavaScript and TypeScript packages with clear boundaries.","headings":[{"id":"why","text":"Why an ecosystem #","level":2},{"id":"scope","text":"What is in scope #","level":2},{"id":"boundaries","text":"What the project will not promise #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Project vision Document Project vision A cohesive ecosystem of focused packages — not one oversized dependency. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Why an ecosystem # Node.js has a mature package ecosystem. It also has recurring friction: overlapping packages, inconsistent contracts, undocumented limits, and security posture that varies by maintainer. Ametherian investigates whether a cohesive family of focused packages can reduce that friction without forcing adoption of one oversized dependency. The goal is not a single framework. The goal is a set of modules that share conventions, document their behavior, and can be adopted independently. What is in scope # The package map groups candidate work into five areas: networking, data and serialization, application infrastructure, security engineering, and observability. Focused responsibilities. A package should do one understandable thing and remain useful without unrelated modules. Explicit limits. Runtime requirements, side effects, failure modes, and resource bounds should be documented rather than hidden. Security by design. Threat models and safe defaults belong in the design, not in a later checklist. What the project will not promise # Complete vulnerability prevention. Universal compatibility with arbitrary applications or runtimes. Automatic safety for applications that misuse or misconfigure a package. Drop-in replacement of Axios, Undici, or any other existing library. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici"},{"path":"/docs/packages/","title":"Package map","section":"Proposal document","description":"The Ametherian package map: networking, data and serialization, application infrastructure, security engineering, and observability research areas.","headings":[{"id":"networking-and-protocols","text":"Networking and protocols #","level":2},{"id":"data-and-serialization","text":"Data and serialization #","level":2},{"id":"application-infrastructure","text":"Application infrastructure #","level":2},{"id":"security-engineering","text":"Security engineering #","level":2},{"id":"observability-and-developer-experience","text":"Observability and developer experience #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Package map Document Package map Five research areas. Candidate packages, not released software. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ The package map is a research agenda. Candidate names and capabilities remain provisional until specifications, threat models, maintenance costs, and implementation feasibility are reviewed. Networking and protocols # HTTP clients, transports, connection pools, proxies, streaming, retries, deadlines, and cancellation. Data and serialization # Validation, schemas, parsing, serialization, conversion, structured data, and safe handling of untrusted input. Application infrastructure # Caching, queues, concurrency, rate limiting, configuration, lifecycle management, and resilience. Security engineering # Security policy helpers, secure configuration, dependency review, test fixtures, and regression tooling. Observability and developer experience # Logging, diagnostics, metrics, tracing, typed errors, test utilities, fuzzing helpers, and documentation tooling. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Project vision Next Architecture →"},{"path":"/docs/architecture/","title":"Architecture direction","section":"Proposal document","description":"Ametherian architecture direction: modular packages, shared contracts, explicit Node.js runtime boundaries, and resource safety.","headings":[{"id":"modularity","text":"Modular packages #","level":2},{"id":"contracts","text":"Shared contracts #","level":2},{"id":"runtime","text":"Runtime boundaries #","level":2},{"id":"safety","text":"Resource safety #","level":2},{"id":"evidence","text":"Evidence-backed compatibility #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Architecture Document Architecture direction Modular packages, explicit runtime boundaries, shared contracts. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Modular packages # Packages should have one understandable responsibility, declare supported runtime capabilities, and remain useful without unrelated modules. Shared contracts # Candidate shared contracts include typed errors, cancellation, deadlines, resource lifecycle, configuration validation, logging hooks, and documentation of side effects. Shared contracts should reduce fragmentation without circular dependencies or forced dependencies. Runtime boundaries # Node.js is the initial target. APIs should make runtime assumptions explicit and isolate environment-specific behavior behind adapters where useful. Unsupported capabilities should fail clearly. Resource safety # Network and stream-oriented packages should define timeouts, maximum sizes, concurrency bounds, backpressure, cancellation, and cleanup. Defaults should be conservative and configurable. Evidence-backed compatibility # Before stable releases, the project should document supported Node.js versions, module formats, TypeScript support, semantic versioning policy, deprecation windows, and interoperability expectations. Compatibility and performance claims need reproducible tests and benchmark methodology. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Package map Next Securi"},{"path":"/docs/security/","title":"Security approach","section":"Proposal document","description":"The Ametherian security approach: threat modeling, secure engineering practices, vulnerability intelligence, and coordinated disclosure.","headings":[{"id":"design","text":"Security by design #","level":2},{"id":"threat-modeling","text":"Threat modeling #","level":2},{"id":"practices","text":"Secure engineering practices #","level":2},{"id":"intelligence","text":"Vulnerability intelligence #","level":2},{"id":"disclosure","text":"Coordinated disclosure #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Security approach Document Security approach Threat models, bounded resources, and evidence-backed claims. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Security by design # Security is a design and maintenance requirement, not a marketing guarantee. Each package must document trust boundaries, threat model, assumptions, and known limitations. Threat modeling # Threat modeling should consider SSRF, DNS rebinding, redirect policy bypasses, TLS hostname and certificate validation, credential exposure, unsafe parsing, prototype pollution, decompression bombs, unbounded resource use, cache poisoning and isolation, filesystem traversal, concurrency races, and dependency or build-chain compromise. Secure engineering practices # Reusing mature cryptographic, TLS, compression, and parsing primitives. Bounding resource use and avoiding secret logging. Validating redirects and protocol transitions. Testing malformed and adversarial inputs. Tracking dependency provenance and adding regression tests for confirmed vulnerabilities. Requiring human review for security-sensitive fixes. Vulnerability intelligence # Vulnerability intelligence may draw on OSV, GitHub Security Advisories, NVD, CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities, Node.js security advisories, and upstream advisories. Any ingestion must preserve source attribution, timestamps, affected-version ranges, confidence, and freshness. Intelligence informs review and testing; it must not trigger unreviewed production changes. Coordinated disclosure # Security reports must follow the security policy . Do not post e"},{"path":"/docs/roadmap/","title":"Roadmap","section":"Proposal document","description":"The staged Ametherian roadmap: research, specifications, implementation, and release readiness.","headings":[{"id":"research","text":"Research and project foundation #","level":2},{"id":"specifications","text":"Specifications and threat models #","level":2},{"id":"implementation","text":"Implementation and verification #","level":2},{"id":"readiness","text":"Release readiness #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Roadmap Document Roadmap Research, specifications, implementation, and release readiness. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ The roadmap is staged and deliberately avoids dates until scope, ownership, and capacity are established. Research and project foundation # Establish the repository, documentation, and initial research areas. Review existing libraries and identify where consistent contracts would help. Specifications and threat models # Write explicit specifications for candidate packages. Define threat models, resource bounds, failure modes, and support matrices. Implementation and verification # Implement packages against the specifications. Require tests, fuzzing where appropriate, and security review before a package can be described as stable. Release readiness # A package is not stable or production-ready until its API, support matrix, documentation, tests, security review, release process, and maintenance ownership are explicit. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Security approach Next Security policy →"},{"path":"/security/","title":"Security policy","section":"Project policy","description":"Ametherian security policy: reporting vulnerabilities, and the intended security process for future packages.","headings":[{"id":"reporting","text":"Reporting a vulnerability #","level":2},{"id":"scope","text":"Scope #","level":2},{"id":"process","text":"Intended process #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Security policy Policy Security policy Reporting status and the intended security process. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ This policy applies to the planning repository and will apply to future implementation repositories. Reporting a vulnerability # Do not open a public issue for a security vulnerability. Report it privately to the maintainers. Security-sensitive details must not be posted publicly before coordinated disclosure. Scope # There are currently no released packages and no security-audited guarantees. If a vulnerability is found in the site infrastructure or repository configuration, it should be reported following this policy. Intended process # Acknowledge receipt of the report. Investigate and confirm the issue. Develop and test a fix. Coordinate disclosure and credit. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Roadmap Next Contributing →"},{"path":"/contributing/","title":"Contributing","section":"Project policy","description":"How to contribute feedback to Ametherian: standards, pull request expectations, and links to the code of conduct.","headings":[{"id":"feedback","text":"Giving feedback #","level":2},{"id":"pull-requests","text":"Pull requests #","level":2},{"id":"security","text":"Security-sensitive changes #","level":2}],"text":"Home / Documentation / Contributing Policy Contributing How to give feedback on the direction and architecture. Reviewed November 2026 · Source repository ↗ Early feedback on scope, package boundaries, security requirements, runtime compatibility, and developer experience is welcome. Giving feedback # Open an issue to discuss proposals, architecture decisions, or documentation gaps. Keep discussions focused on the technical direction and evidence. Pull requests # Pull requests should be small, focused, and accompanied by a clear description of the problem and the proposed solution. Documentation and tests are part of the change. Security-sensitive changes # Do not post exploit details publicly. Follow the security policy for vulnerability reports and security-sensitive fixes. Independent project. Ametherian is not affiliated with or endorsed by Node.js, the OpenJS Foundation, Axios, Undici, or any other referenced project. Node.js is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. Previous ← Security policy"},{"path":"/sitemap/","title":"XML sitemap","section":"Site index","description":"Browse the public pages and documentation in the Ametherian project.","headings":[],"text":"Ametherian · Site index XML Sitemap Explore the public pages, project policies, and technical documentation that make up Ametherian. Filter sitemap pages View raw XML Loading the sitemap… Enable JavaScript to display the visual sitemap. The XML sitemap remains available . The XML file remains the authoritative crawler-facing sitemap. This page transforms that same XML locally in your browser."}];
